      "text": "We are given the parametric equations $(x, y) = (2\\sin t, 2\\cos t)$ that describe a unit circle centered at the origin in the Cartesian plane.\n\nWe start with $\\dfrac{x}{2} = \\sin t$, and from here, we determine that $t = \\arcsin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)$. \n\nNext, we use $\\dfrac{y}{2} = \\cos t$ to write the definite integral from $0$ to $\\pi$:\n\\[\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left[\\cos t - \\cos^{2} t\\right] dt\\]\n\nNow, we come to the point where we evaluate the expression. Utilizing trigonometric identities, $\\sin^{2}t + \\cos^{2}t = 1$ to rewrite $\\cos^{2} t$:\n\\[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int (1 - \\sin^{2}t)dt \\]\n\nNext, treating $\\sin^{2}t$ as $u$:\n\\[\\dfrac{1}{2} \\int (1 - u) \\,du \\]\n\nNow, we integrate with both the $u$ terms:\n\\[= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left[u - \\dfrac{u^{2}}{2}\\right] \\]\n\nFinally, substituting $u = \\sin^{2} t$ back in the equation:\n\\[= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left[ \\sin^{2} t - \\dfrac{ \\sin^{4} t}{2} \\right] \\]\n\nPutting this back into the original inequality, we get:\n\\[= \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin^{2} t - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin^{4} t \\right] \\,dt \\]\n\nThus, the final expression is:\n\\[\\ldots \\boxed{\\dfrac{\\sin^{4} t}{4} - \\dfrac{\\sin^{2} t}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{4}} \\]",
