      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve \\((x,y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we can use the formula for the arc length of a parametric curve \\((x(t), y(t))\\):\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(x(t) = 2 \\sin t\\) and \\(y(t) = 2 \\cos t\\). So, \\(\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t\\).\n\nThe parameters \\(a = 0\\) and \\(b = \\pi\\), so we need to compute:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nEvaluating this integral, we get:\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 1 \\, dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this result using Python and sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the parameter t\nt = sp.symbols('t')\n\n# Define the parametric equations\nx = 2 * sp.sin(t)\ny = 2 * sp.cos(t)\n\n# Compute the derivatives\ndx_dt = sp.diff(x, t)\ndy_dt = sp.diff(y, t)\n\n# Compute the integrand of the arc length formula\nintegrand = sp.sqrt(dx_dt**2 + dy_dt**2)\n\n# Compute the arc length\narc_length = sp.integrate(integrand, (t, 0, sp.pi))\nprint(arc_length)\n```\n```output\n2*pi\n```\nThe length of the parametric curve \\((x,y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).",
