      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve described by \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we use the arc length formula for parametric curves. The formula is given by:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nHere, \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\), so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t, \\quad \\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nFirst, we compute:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 = (2 \\cos t)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 t\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2 = (-2 \\sin t)^2 = 4 \\sin^2 t\n\\]\n\nThe integrand becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} = \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} = \\sqrt{4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)} = \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 1} = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n\\]\n\nSo the integral for the length is:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nWe can now evaluate this integral:\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 2 \\left( \\pi - 0 \\right) = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the parametric curve is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi}\n\\]",
